# ord-ppl-v1 — the lower-is-better half of the comparison lookup table
#
# S2's ord-v1 and S3's ord-le-v1 both compare accuracy, where a larger number is
# the better result. This policy compares PERPLEXITY, where a smaller number is.
# It exists because that difference is invisible to everything except a
# declaration: as propositions, num_lt(0.71, 0.74) and num_lt(28.4, 31.6) are
# the same shape, and nothing in an artifact says which of the two systems the
# smaller number flatters.
#
# The direction lives in the RULES, and this is the whole design:
#
#   * ppl_recheck — STRICT. Its conclusion is num_lt(Sv, Bv) — OURS below
#     THEIRS — where ord-v1's beats_recheck concludes num_lt(Bv, Sv). Same
#     backend, same certificate shape, opposite argument order, because on
#     perplexity "we are lower" is the good news.
#
#   * ppl_bridge — DEFEASIBLE. Consumes that strict conclusion plus the binding,
#     and concludes better(S, B, Q, D) — the SAME comparative predicate ord-v1's
#     bridge concludes. The reader's claim is "sys_new is better on perplexity";
#     it should not be spelled differently just because the metric runs the other
#     way.
#
# What S5 pins that S2 and S3 cannot: the elaborator does not flip an argument
# order on its own. `measurand perplexity : Num where lower-is-better` selects
# the (strictly-better, lower-is-better) cell of the comparison lookup table,
# and that cell resolves to THESE rules, whose premises and conclusion are
# welded together by shared variables. So the generated goal's argument order,
# the rule that consumes it, and the comparative claim it licenses cannot
# disagree — flipping one without the others is not expressible.
#
# Polarity is declared, never inferred, and S5 is the reason. Given only this
# artifact — two reported cells and an attestation — no analysis recovers the
# fact that lower perplexity is better. That is knowledge about perplexity.

policy ord-ppl-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S5/ord-ppl-v1.policy.lara examples/S5/example.lara

sort Experiment, Cell, System, Measurand, Dataset

con exp1 : Experiment
con perplexity : Measurand
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Cell
con sys_base : System
con sys_new : System
con wikitext103 : Dataset

pred better(System, System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred comparison_setup(System, System, Measurand, Dataset, Num, Num)
pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred reports(Experiment, Cell)

# --- The strict leg: the certificate-checked ordered comparison -----------------------------
# Premise order fixes slot order, and the generated certificate cites it: (prem 0) is the SYSTEM
# cell, (prem 1) the BASELINE cell — the mirror image of ord-v1, where slot 0 is the baseline.
# The conclusion num_lt(Sv, Bv) says system < baseline, i.e. the system scores lower on a
# measurand where lower is better.
#
# As in ord-v1, both compared values trace to a consulted premise (design §2.4): the baseline
# perplexity is not a bare goal constant, it enters as a leaf feeding a premise slot.

rule ppl_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)),
                 reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Sv, Bv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-ppl-v1-theory-0) ]

# The empty theory the ord@1 certificates cite, as in ord-v1: the certified comparison is pure
# arithmetic, so the theory contributes no entries and the digest is selection identity only.

theory sha256:ord-ppl-v1-theory-0 = []

# --- The bridge: from a bare comparison to a comparative claim ------------------------------
# Structurally ord-v1's beats_baseline with the arithmetic premise mirrored. The binding premise
# is NOT mirrored: comparison_setup names the system's value first regardless of polarity, which
# is what lets one attestation vocabulary serve both directions.

rule ppl_bridge(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ cmp:     num_lt(Sv, Bv),
                 binding: comparison_setup(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv) ]
  conclusion = better(S, B, Q, D)

# --- The @0.3 comparison surface ------------------------------------------------------------
# The one declaration that distinguishes this policy from ord-v1 at the surface. Everything
# above could have been written at @0.2; this line is what makes the direction machine-readable.

measurand perplexity : Num where lower-is-better

# The same `relation = strictly-better` an author writes in S2 resolves HERE to ppl_recheck and
# ppl_bridge, because the scheme key is the pair and this policy declares the lower-is-better
# member of it. S2's source and S5's source state the same research relation; the generated
# goals are mirror images. That is the polarity contract, executing.

comparison-scheme strictly-better lower-is-better
  recheck = ppl_recheck
  bridge  = ppl_bridge

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the only strict rule is ppl_recheck, whose conclusion
# pattern num_lt(Sv, Bv) heads no contrary pair, so no contrary side is strict-reachable and the
# policy passes the R12 check — ord-v1's argument, unchanged by the mirrored argument order. The
# intra-family exclusion lemmas in lean/Lara/Ord.lean are stated over the literals, not over a
# direction convention, so they cover this policy exactly as they cover ord-v1's.
